---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import CopyCommand from '../components/CopyCommand.astro'

const install = 'curl -fsSL https://github.com/anton-fomin/ws/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh'
---

<Base
  title="ws: one branch, every repo"
  description="ws checks a branch out in every repository of a project, runs each repo's setup, and lays the result out in tmux or herdr."
>
  <section class="hero container">
    <div class="hero__text">
      <h1>One branch, every repo.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        One command checks a branch out in every repository of a project, runs each repo's setup, and lays the result
        out in tmux or herdr.
      </p>
      <CopyCommand command={install} />
      <p class="hero__actions">
        <a class="button button--primary" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
        <a class="button" href="https://github.com/anton-fomin/ws">GitHub</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="terminal hero__demo">
      <div class="terminal__bar" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
      <video
        autoplay
        muted
        loop
        playsinline
        preload="metadata"
        poster="/demo/poster.png"
        width="960"
        height="600"
        aria-label="ws init, ws switch feature/login opening a tmux session, and ws run dev in both repositories"
      >
        <source src="/demo/demo.webm" type="video/webm" />
      </video>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p>
          A feature touches the API and the web app. So you <code>git switch</code> in one repository, then the other,
          then you remember the infrastructure repo. Halfway through a review someone needs a hotfix, and every one of
          them has to move again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Worktrees help, but one repository at a time: each new one starts without your <code>.env</code>,
          without <code>node_modules</code>, and without the setup steps nobody wrote down.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre><code><span class="dim"># without ws, for every branch</span>
cd ~/src/api && git switch -c feature/login
cd ~/src/web && git switch -c feature/login
cd ~/src/infra && git switch -c feature/login
cp ../.env.backup .env  <span class="dim"># which one was it?</span>
pnpm install           <span class="dim"># again</span></code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p class="section__lede">
      A workspace is a directory with a <code>ws.yaml</code>. It holds one bare clone per repository and one branch
      directory per branch, with a worktree of every repository inside. Which branch is where is always read back from
      git.
    </p>
    <div class="terminal layout">
      <pre><code><span class="dir">acme/</span>
  <span class="file">ws.yaml</span>                <span class="dim"># repositories, setup, overrides</span>
  <span class="dir">.bare/</span>api.git  <span class="dir">.bare/</span>web.git   <span class="dim"># one bare clone per repository</span>
  <span class="dir">local/</span>api/.env         <span class="dim"># untracked files for every worktree</span>
  <span class="dir">main/</span>api   <span class="dir">main/</span>web       <span class="dim"># a branch directory per branch…</span>
  <span class="dir accent">feature-login/</span>api      <span class="dim"># …with one worktree per repository</span>
  <span class="dir accent">feature-login/</span>web</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>ws switch feature/login</code> creates the worktrees, places your local files, installs dependencies, runs
      each repository's setup and opens the session. <a href="/docs/layout/">More on the layout →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Features</h2>
    <ul class="features">
      <li>
        <strong>One feature folder, every repo.</strong> Create worktrees side by side in <code>project/feature/</code>,
        with <code>backend/</code> and <code>frontend/</code> inside.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Packages installed automatically.</strong> Works with uv, npm, pnpm and Yarn.
        pnpm and Yarn reuse dependencies through hardlinks where possible.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Remote branches, ready locally.</strong> Fetch all repos with <code>ws fetch</code>,
        then switch to new worktrees from a remote branch.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run tasks across repos.</strong> Define <code>dev</code> in a Taskfile, justfile or <code>package.json</code>,
        then run <code>ws run dev</code>. All repos run in parallel, with output in one console.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Logs your agent can read.</strong> Enable file logs for all tasks run with <code>ws run</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local files.</strong> Copy or link <code>.env</code>, editor settings and other files
        from a <code>local/</code> folder into your worktrees.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your setup commands.</strong> Run custom commands on worktree creation—for example,
        decrypt SOPS secrets into a <code>.env</code> file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>For people who love the CLI.</strong> Open a tmux session or herdr workspace for your branch,
        with a window or tab per repo and a shared root for multi-repo workspaces.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Agent skills included.</strong> Let your coding agent create worktrees, find its way around and run tasks.</li>
      <li><strong>Written in Rust.</strong> Blazingly fast, of course.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <code>git</code> is the only requirement. tmux, herdr, fzf, zoxide and package managers are optional integrations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Works with coding agents</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p>
          Type <code>/ws &lt;task&gt;</code> in Claude Code or OpenCode, or <code>/skill:ws &lt;task&gt;</code> in pi.
          The agent picks a branch name from your
          repositories' conventions, runs <code>ws switch</code>, and starts a fresh agent on the task in the new
          workspace's session.
        </p>
        <p>
          A second skill teaches every agent inside a workspace how to find its way around: <code>ws status</code>,
          each repository's own instructions, <code>ws run</code>
          for tasks. <a href="/docs/coding-agents/">Coding agents →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <CopyCommand command="npx skills add anton-fomin/ws -g -a claude-code opencode" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <div class="install-options">
      <div>
        <h3>Linux and macOS</h3>
        <CopyCommand command={install} />
        <p>A prebuilt binary in <code>~/.local/bin</code>, checked against the release's checksum. No sudo.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Nix</h3>
        <CopyCommand command="nix profile add github:anton-fomin/ws" />
        <p>Or as a flake input, system-wide or in a project's dev shell.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Then add the shell wrapper so <code>ws switch</code> also changes directory, and shell
      completion. <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Getting started →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 3.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 6fr);
      align-items: center;
      padding-top: 5rem;
    }
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 9vw, 3.5rem);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--ws-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
  }

  .hero__demo {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .hero__demo video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    background: var(--ws-bg-inset);
  }

  .section {
    padding-top: 4rem;
  }

  .section h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .section h2::before {
    content: '## ';
    color: var(--ws-accent);
  }

  .section__lede {
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  .two-col {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .two-col {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 2.5rem;
    }
  }

  .two-col p:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .dim {
    color: var(--ws-text-muted);
  }

  .dir {
    color: var(--ws-term-blue);
  }

  .dir.accent {
    color: var(--ws-accent);
  }

  .file {
    color: var(--ws-term-yellow);
  }

  .layout {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .features {
    max-width: 78ch;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ws-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .features li {
    padding-left: 0.35rem;
  }

  .features li + li {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
  }

  .features li::marker {
    color: var(--ws-accent);
  }

  .features strong {
    color: var(--ws-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .features code {
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    color: var(--ws-text);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .note {
    max-width: 78ch;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--ws-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .install-options {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .install-options h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .install-options p {
    color: var(--ws-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
